STRCMP(3)                      P32W Unix                       STRCMP(3)

NAME
       strcmp, strncmp, strcoll - compare strings

SYNOPSIS
       #include <string.h>

       int strcmp(const char *a, const char *b);
       int strncmp(const char *a, const char *b, size_t n);
       int strcoll(const char *a, const char *b);

DESCRIPTION
       strcmp compares the strings a and b character by character,
       each taken as an unsigned char (so the order is ASCII's,
       with bytes 128 to 255 after it). strncmp compares at most the
       first n characters. strcoll compares in the order of the
       locale; P32W Unix has only the "C" locale, where it is strcmp.

RETURN VALUE
       0 if the strings are equal (in their first n characters, for
       strncmp); otherwise the difference of the first two
       characters that differ: negative if a sorts before b,
       positive if after. A string sorts before any longer string
       that starts with it.

EXAMPLES
       if (strcmp(argv[1], "-l") == 0)
           ...
       if (strncmp(line, "From ", 5) == 0)
           ...

SEE ALSO
       string(3), memory(3), qsort(3)

BUGS
       There is no strcasecmp or strcoll: case matters, and there is
       no locale.
